The five principal Balboan groups — Black, Mixed, Sinchew, White, and Zong — account for approximately 81 per cent of the population. The remaining 19 per cent comprises immigrant communities and smaller ethnic groups, a proportion that is significantly higher than the national average and reflects the city's role as a financial hub and port city.

[[Black Balboan people|Black Balboans]] are the largest group at approximately 35 per cent, a proportion that reflects the city's history as the centre of the [[trans-Kaftian slave trade]] and the subsequent concentration of the Black population in the urban Estret corridor. The Black community is the majority in [[Original (ciutat)|Original]]'s southern neighbourhoods and [[Barcelona (ciutat)|Barcelona]], and forms a significant presence in [[Sant Llorenç (ciutat)|Sant Llorenç]] and [[Ciutat de l'Estació (ciutat)|Ciutat de l'Estació]].

[[Mixed Balboan people|Mixed Balboans]] at 20 per cent are distributed throughout the city, with particular concentrations in the residential ciutats of [[Sant Jordi (ciutat)|Sant Jordi]] and [[Sant Martí (ciutat)|Sant Martí]].

[[Sinchewese Balboan people|Sinchew Balboans]] at 10 per cent are the largest Fosian subgroup, reflecting the Sinchewese community's long history in the city as agricultural workers and, later, commercial and professional residents. The Sinchewese community is concentrated in [[Original (ciutat)|Original]]'s eastern neighbourhoods (the former [[Barri de l'Est]]) and in [[Dàrsena (ciutat)|Dàrsena]].

[[White Balboan people|White Balboans]] at 9 per cent are significantly underrepresented compared to the national average of 26 per cent, reflecting the white community's historical concentration in the interior provinces. The white population in Sant Cristòfor is concentrated in the affluent ciutats of [[Costa Nord (ciutat)|Costa Nord]], [[Sant Miquel (ciutat)|Sant Miquel]], and [[Mare de Déu del Mar (ciutat)|Mare de Déu del Mar]], and in the financial sector professional class of Original's bay shore.

[[Zong Balboan people|Zong Balboans]] at 7 per cent are the second Fosian subgroup. The Zong community, which dominated the city during the [[Zong Balboa]] period, declined sharply after the [[Great Adonian War]] and has never recovered its pre-war prominence. The remaining Zong Balboan population is concentrated in Original's eastern neighbourhoods and in the commercial and professional sectors, and is distinct from the more recent Zong immigrant community (see below).

=== Immigrant communities ===

The city's immigrant population — approximately 19 per cent of the total — is the largest of any Balboan city and reflects Sant Cristòfor's position as a financial hub and port city at the junction of the Illypnian, Fosian, and Kaftian markets.

The largest immigrant communities are from [[Dagit]] (~3 per cent), [[Sanese people|Sanese]] (~2 per cent), [[Burawi people|Burawi]] (~2 per cent), and [[Mercia|Mercian]] (~2 per cent). The Dagit community is concentrated in the working-class ciutats and is employed primarily in the service, logistics, and construction sectors. The Burawi community, by contrast, is predominantly professional and working-class, with a significant presence in the film and media industry — a legacy of the Burawi filmmakers and technicians who fled the [[Burawi socialist revolution]] of the 1930s and settled in [[Costa Nord (ciutat)|Costa Nord]], where they helped establish the city's film industry. The Sanese community is predominantly commercial and professional. The Mercian community is concentrated in the financial sector and the international institutions.

A small but notable recent Zong immigrant community — distinct from the multigenerational [[Zong Balboan people|Zong Balboan]] population — has established itself in the city since the 2000s, drawn by the financial sector and the city's role as a regional commercial hub. The recent Zong immigrants are overwhelmingly professional-class — bankers, lawyers, engineers, and corporate managers — and are concentrated in Original's bay shore and the financial district, a contrast with the historically working-class and commercial Zong Balboan community of the eastern neighbourhoods.

Smaller but notable communities include [[Birú people|Birú and other indigenous]] peoples (~1 per cent, primarily internal migrants from the rural interior), [[Breislandic people|Breislandic]] Illypnians (~0.5 per cent, concentrated in the financial sector and international organisations), [[Midlandic people|Midlandic]] (~0.5 per cent, from the [[United Midlands]], concentrated in the financial sector and international organisations), [[Daras|Darasian]] (~0.5 per cent), [[Chatari people|Chatar]] Central Fosians (~0.5 per cent), [[Pangkajenan people|Pangkajene]] North Fosians (~0.5 per cent), and [[Laga people|Laga]] North Kaftians (~0.5 per cent).

=== Income ===

{{See also|Economy of Sant Cristòfor}}

Median household income in the metropolitan area is approximately $22,400 — more than three times the national median of $6,700 — but income inequality within the city is significant. The Gini coefficient for the metropolitan area is approximately 46, driven by the contrast between the high-income professional class in Original, Barcelona, and Costa Nord, and the lower-income service and industrial workforce in Sant Llorenç, Ciutat de l'Estació, and Sant Miquel.

