[[FC Sant Cristòfor]] is the city's oldest and most successful club, with 11 La Lliga titles. Founded in 1928, the club is associated with the port and the commercial class. Its rivalry with [[Club Esportiu Sant Bart]] — the [[Clàssic de l'Istme]] — is the most intense in Balboan football. [[Unió Esportiva del Mercat]] is the club of the city's historic Black quarter, named after the covered market in the Barri Negre neighbourhood; its identity is rooted in the Black Balboan community and Creole-language cultural expression. [[Club Esportiu Haimêng]] takes its name from the Hokkien 海門 (''Hái-mn̂g'', "Sea Gate"), the Zong name for the old harbour neighbourhood; it is based in Dàrsena. [[Club Esportiu de l'Est]] originated in the Sinchewese community's Barri de l'Est (Eastern Quarter). [[Promoció FC]], based in Costa Nord, was promoted to La Lliga for the first time in 2024.

Sant Cristòfor was one of the host cities for the [[1994 AFA World Cup]], hosted by [[Balboa]]. The tournament was a landmark event for the city, prompting investment in stadium infrastructure and urban improvements that contributed to the broader transformation of the 2000s.

=== Other sports ===

Beyond football, Sant Cristòfor maintains professional clubs in several other sports. In {{w|baseball}}, the city is represented by [[Barcelona BC]] in [[Barcelona (ciutat)|Barcelona]] and [[Original BC]] in [[Original (ciutat)|Original]], both competing in the [[Balboan Baseball League]]. In {{w|basketball}}, three clubs compete in the [[Lliga de Bàsquet Balboana]]: [[Bàsquet Barcelona]] and [[Bàsquet Original]] — reflecting the same Barcelona–Original rivalry found in the city's cultural life — and [[Bàsquet Dàrsena]]. In {{w|field hockey}}, [[Hoquei Dàrsena]] and [[Hoquei Sant Jordi]] compete in the [[Lliga d'Hoquei Balboana]].
